    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=\nWrite a complete, standalone beginner lesson using the topic,\nlearner profile, and lesson plan provided below.\n\nTOPIC:\n{{ $('Edit Fields').first().json.topic }}\n\nLEARNER PROFILE:\nEducation: {{ $('Edit Fields').first().json.learner_education }}\nEnglish level: {{ $('Edit Fields').first().json.learner_english }}\nTechnical background: {{ $('Edit Fields').first().json.learner_background }}\n\nLESSON PLAN:\n{{ JSON.stringify($('Planner Agent').first().json.output) }}\n\nPAST FAILURES ON THIS TOPIC (memory from previous runs — actively avoid repeating these mistakes):\n{{ $('Read Memory').all().filter(r => r.json.failed_checks).map(r => '- ' + r.json.failed_checks + ': ' + r.json.failure_reason).join('\\n') || 'None recorded yet.' }}\n\nWrite the complete lesson, not another plan.\n\nThe lesson must teach:\n1. What RAG is.\n2. Why RAG is useful.\n3. How RAG works, step by step.\n4. A familiar, concrete example that shows the full process.\n5. The meaning of important technical terms.\n6. A short recap.\n\nUse simple English suitable for a learner who has just completed\n12th grade in India and has no technical background.\n\nTeaching rules:\n- Start with a familiar situation before introducing technical terms.\n- Explain each technical term in simple words when it first appears.\n- Use short sentences and common words.\n- Explain ideas in a logical order.\n- Do not assume the learner knows AI, coding, databases, or embeddings.\n- Use examples to explain difficult ideas.\n- Do not make unsupported claims.\n- Do not skip any important part of the lesson plan.\n- Do not mention the internal workflow, planner, evaluator, or retry process.\n\nReturn only the lesson content.\nDo not return JSON or a lesson plan.",
        "options": {
          "systemMessage": "\nYou are a beginner-friendly educational content writer.\n\nYour task is to teach a topic to a learner with no technical\nbackground and limited English vocabulary.\n\nWrite lessons that are accurate, clear, and easy to understand.\n\nUse simple words, short sentences, familiar examples, and a\nlogical teaching sequence.\n\nExplain technical terms before using them in complex ways.\n\nFollow the supplied lesson plan, but do not copy it as a list\nof headings without teaching the ideas.\n\nWrite a complete lesson that can be understood independently.\n\nNever invent facts to make an explanation sound more convincing."
        }
      },
